{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>They shall live, etc.</b> Only your dead — the kaf is for addressing the Name [directly]; they shall live. And according to everyone's opinion this hints at the revival of the dead. And some say it is the opposite of 'the dead shall not live'; for behold, our lords have died, and we who were accounted as dead are alive." "<b>My corpses.</b> The yod is a sign that the prophet is of Israel. And thus: 'your dead shall live' and 'my corpses shall arise' — as though a voice is heard saying: Awake! And similarly, יחיו מתיך ומתי נבלתי יקומון (your dead shall live, my corpses shall arise), as though a voice is heard saying: Awake!" "<b>For the dew of lights</b> — dew with light; it is your dew. And the kaf is for addressing the Name [directly]. And some say that אורות are plants, as in ללקט אורות (2 Kings 4:39), and they are plants that grow toward the sun and the moon." "<b>And the earth.</b> The vav is opened [with dagesh] to indicate the missing demonstrative heh [i.e., הארץ]. 'And the earth shall cast down the Rephaim from it' — like the construct of אשת. And some say: You alone have given life to your dead, for the nature of the earth is to cast the dead into it."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:55:21.507484065Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}